# {{ proper_name }}

## Build

{% if 'frontend' in features %}
    make            # build the web assets in src/ (runs npm install on first use)
{% end %}
{% if 'web' in features %}
    make run        # run from the source tree on http://127.0.0.1:8080/, or ARGS="--port 9000"
{% else %}
    make run        # run from the source tree, until Ctrl-C or SIGTERM
{% end %}
    make venv       # make .venv with the package, pytest and build, with uv if it's installed
    make test       # run the tests in tests/
    make install    # pip install the package
    make dist       # build the sdist and wheel in dist/

The targets use `.venv` when there is one, or set `PYTHON` to use another Python.

## Configuration

Settings are read from the `[{{ lower_name }}]` section of these ini files, in order:

- `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.ini`
- `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.ini.local`
- `/etc/{{ namespace or lower_name }}/{{ lower_name }}.ini`
- `~/.{{ lower_name }}.ini`
- any file passed with `--ini`
{% if 'auth' in features %}

## Logins

Every page needs a login. Add a user, or change their password, with:

    python3 -m {{ py_name }} --add-user <user>

`--remove-user <user>` removes a user and ends their sessions, and `--list-users` lists them.
Users are kept in `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.users`, and sessions, with the secret
that signs their cookies, in `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.sessions`, so logins survive
a restart. Set `users` and `sessions` in the `[auth]` section of the ini file to keep them
elsewhere, for example when the package is installed. `on_login_success()` in
`{{ py_path }}/{{ lower_name }}.py` decides what goes in a session.
{% end %}
{% if 'docker' in features %}

## Docker

Build the image and start the app in the background:

{% if 'postgres' in features %}
    mkdir -p secrets && openssl rand -hex 24 > secrets/db_password.txt
{% end %}
    docker compose up -d --build
{% if 'auth' in features %}
    docker compose run --rm {{ lower_name }} --add-user <user>
{% end %}

{% if 'web' in features %}
It listens on `127.0.0.1:8080` of this host, or the port in `PORT`, and keeps its files in
{% else %}
It keeps its files in
{% end %}
the `data` volume. `docker/{{ lower_name }}.ini` has the settings for the container, and
`/data/.{{ lower_name }}.ini` in the volume can change them.
{% if 'nginx' in features %}

For nginx on this host, the container prints the site, which sends everything to the app,
static files too. Add `--port` if `PORT` isn't 8080:

    docker compose run --rm -T {{ lower_name }} --nginx <server_name> | sudo tee /etc/nginx/sites-available/<server_name>
{% end %}
{% end %}
{% if 'db' in features %}

## Database

{% if 'sqlite' in features %}
The app opens `{{ py_path }}/data/{{ lower_name }}.db` at startup, or the `path` in the
`[sqlite]` section of the ini file,
{% else %}
The app connects at startup to the PostgreSQL database in the `url` of the `[postgres]`
section of the ini file, `postgresql://localhost/{{ lower_name }}` if it isn't set,
{% end %}
and creates the tables of the models in `{{ py_path }}/models.py` that don't exist yet.
Existing tables aren't changed, so a changed model needs its table altered or dropped by
hand. Handlers use the database with:

    async with db.session() as session:
        messages = await session.scalars(sqlalchemy.select(Message))
{% if 'web' in features %}

`/api/messages` is an example: GET lists the messages as JSON, and POST adds one from a body
like `{"text": "hi"}`.
{% end %}
{% if 'postgres' in features %}

Keep the password out of the ini files with `password_file`, a file that holds only the
password. The tests that need the database run when `TEST_POSTGRES_URL` is set, for example
`postgresql://user:password@localhost/{{ lower_name }}_test`, and skip otherwise.
{% end %}
{% end %}
{% if 'nginx' in features %}

## nginx

Install an nginx site that serves `/static/` itself and proxies the rest, including the
WebSocket, to the app:

    sudo python3 -m {{ py_name }} --nginx <server_name>
    sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/<server_name> /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
    sudo nginx -t && sudo systemctl reload nginx

It redirects HTTP to HTTPS and expects a certificate in `/etc/letsencrypt/live/<server_name>/`,
for example from `certbot certonly`. The `prefix` setting puts the app under a path, so other
sites can share the server. Settings in the `[nginx]` section of the ini file fill in
`{{ py_path }}/data/nginx.conf`.
{% end %}
{% if 'systemd' in features %}

## systemd

Install a service that runs the app from the current directory, then start it:

    sudo python3 -m {{ py_name }} --systemd [<user>]
    sudo systemctl daemon-reload
    sudo systemctl enable --now {{ lower_name }}

The service runs as `<user>`, or the user who ran `sudo`, with the same Python and
`--ini` file. Settings in the `[systemd]` section of the ini file fill in
`{{ py_path }}/data/systemd.service`.
{% end %}
